title: Why there is no ref-list panel
tags: [[Panel types]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: "What points at this node?" used to be a panel type of its own; it is now a query the treeref panel writes, because an answer whose query is hidden can neither be narrowed nor combined.

Until September 2026 a `ref-list` panel listed the metarecords pointing at the node
selected in `treeref`. It was removed (`b4755563`) for two reasons:

* it cost a whole panel type — its own display, its own keys — for one question about another panel's selection;
* its answer was a dead end: the query behind it was never shown, so the user could not narrow it, sort it by a column of their choice, or combine it with another condition.

The [[treeref panel]] writes the query instead, and says which before running it: its
ref bar shows `tag -> (path = "music/jazz")` for the node under the cursor.
`treeref:list-refs` hands that text to [[metarecord-list panel]] through
`metarecord-list:query-request` and opens the list in the ''other'' slot — the pairing
`ref-list` provided. It lands in the normal DSL, shown and frozen: a starting point,
not a mode. The same choice is made by `metarecord-list:folder` and
`metarecord-list:query`.

The ''exact'' scope is only right since `=` on a `tree_ref` field means the node at
that path ([[Field aspects]]). Before, `path = "music"` matched every node ''named''
`music`, at any depth, and no query could name one node.
